WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.564 --> 00:00:06.285
Hello, everyone!

2
00:00:06.766 --> 00:00:17.934
Welcome back to Superstars in History.

3
00:00:18.475 --> 00:00:26.621
I'm your host, Faye Moss, and today's guest was a brilliant researcher who won not one but two

4
00:00:26.861 --> 00:00:30.824
Nobel Prizes for her work in physics and chemistry.

5
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:36.263
She's also the first woman ever to win a Nobel Prize.

6
00:00:37.363 --> 00:00:42.266
Let's welcome a true science superstar, Marie Curie!

7
00:01:00.055 --> 00:01:01.216
Thank you, Faye.

8
00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:07.123
I must confess I'd rather be back in a science lab, but I appreciate this opportunity to visit

9
00:01:07.163 --> 00:01:12.986
you! We're excited to learn more about your groundbreaking research, Dr.

10
00:01:13.046 --> 00:01:16.668
Curie. But first let's talk about your childhood.

11
00:01:17.569 --> 00:01:23.992
You were born in 1867 in Warsaw, Poland, and named Maria Sklodowska, right?

12
00:01:25.053 --> 00:01:28.214
Yes, and I was always a very serious student.

13
00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:35.709
My father, who was an educator, immersed our family in learning, teaching us several languages as well as

14
00:01:35.769 --> 00:01:37.250
geography and poetry.

15
00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:44.432
Because Father taught science, there was also scientific equipment around our home, and I learned how to use

16
00:01:44.532 --> 00:01:45.692
it at an early age.

17
00:01:46.712 --> 00:01:51.033
Oh, and I graduated high school at age 15, first in my class.

18
00:01:52.054 --> 00:01:56.535
Whoa! I'm not surprised you were a spectacular student.

19
00:01:57.335 --> 00:02:04.114
But as a female growing up in the nineteenth century, you encountered at least a few obstacles on

20
00:02:04.154 --> 00:02:05.254
your career path.

21
00:02:06.375 --> 00:02:13.758
Indeed! I desperately wanted to attend university, but educational opportunities in Poland were limited.

22
00:02:14.779 --> 00:02:17.580
Women weren't allowed to enroll at universities.

23
00:02:18.601 --> 00:02:24.083
Poland was occupied by Russia at the time, and the Russian government also made it difficult for many

24
00:02:24.163 --> 00:02:25.524
Poles to get an education.

25
00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:33.743
So my sister Bronya and I joined a secret organization called the Flying University, which met every night

26
00:02:33.803 --> 00:02:40.706
in different locations to educate women in anatomy, natural history, and yes, Polish culture.

27
00:02:41.846 --> 00:02:46.228
The sessions were interesting, but they weren't enough for me or Bronya.

28
00:02:47.328 --> 00:02:55.072
Eventually the two of us hatched a plan—we'd leave Poland and go to the Sorbonne, a university in

29
00:02:55.172 --> 00:02:57.824
Paris. Great idea!

30
00:02:58.304 --> 00:02:59.565
Did you go to school together?

31
00:02:59.585 --> 00:03:05.266
No, we couldn't afford that, so we decided that Bronya would go first.

32
00:03:06.247 --> 00:03:13.389
Meanwhile I stayed in Poland and worked as a governess, sending her money to help fund her education.

33
00:03:13.549 --> 00:03:21.431
In 1891 it was finally my turn, and I enrolled at the Sorbonne as "Marie," the French version

34
00:03:21.471 --> 00:03:22.151
of my name.

35
00:03:23.311 --> 00:03:28.681
At the Sorbonne, you earned two degrees—one in physics and one in math.

36
00:03:29.722 --> 00:03:30.462
That's correct.

37
00:03:31.382 --> 00:03:36.505
And even though I was very poor as a student, it was one of the most perfect times

38
00:03:36.565 --> 00:03:39.786
of my life—a new world had opened to me.

39
00:03:40.907 --> 00:03:44.588
Yet even in Paris, women experienced discrimination.

40
00:03:45.429 --> 00:03:48.910
Females couldn't vote and few attended university.

41
00:03:50.291 --> 00:03:52.652
Even fewer conducted scientific research.

42
00:03:53.640 --> 00:03:57.403
In fact, no female in France had ever earned a doctorate.

43
00:03:58.564 --> 00:04:06.550
Speaking of research, you eventually acquired a famous lab partner your husband, Pierre Curie!

44
00:04:06.991 --> 00:04:10.133
How did you two meet?

45
00:04:10.233 --> 00:04:15.738
In 1894 I had a job studying the magnetic properties of steel and needed lab space.

46
00:04:16.839 --> 00:04:23.264
Friends introduced me to Pierre Curie, another physicist, thinking he might be able to find me a space.

47
00:04:24.292 --> 00:04:27.935
Shortly afterward we fell in love and married in 1895.

48
00:04:28.075 --> 00:04:31.638
It was a simple ceremony, of course.

49
00:04:32.659 --> 00:04:37.663
I wore a navy blue suit—a practical outfit that I could later wear to the lab.

50
00:04:38.684 --> 00:04:43.568
Your research led to some important prizes, which we'll definitely discuss.

51
00:04:44.429 --> 00:04:47.391
For now, can you tell us more about your research?

52
00:04:48.692 --> 00:04:55.529
I had become interested in the work of a French scientist, Henri Becquerel, who'd discovered that rocks containing

53
00:04:55.629 --> 00:04:58.129
uranium emit light or rays.

54
00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:05.571
I wanted to explore whether other chemical bodies had this property, which I called radioactivity.

55
00:05:06.652 --> 00:05:13.434
Using a device that measured electrical changes in the air, I found that the element thorium also emitted

56
00:05:13.494 --> 00:05:15.974
rays. Did you find others?

57
00:05:17.095 --> 00:05:19.195
Oh yes, and Pierre joined me.

58
00:05:20.135 --> 00:05:27.125
We discovered two new elements: polonium, which I named after my homeland of Poland, and radium.

59
00:05:28.086 --> 00:05:32.050
To our surprise, radium was even more radioactive than uranium.

60
00:05:33.011 --> 00:05:37.715
When we isolated some larger pieces of it, we saw that they glowed!

61
00:05:38.917 --> 00:05:42.040
We did this work in an old shed with broken windows.

62
00:05:43.061 --> 00:05:45.944
We burned our fingers and suffered from exhaustion.

63
00:05:47.140 --> 00:05:52.562
Luckily you were able to publish papers about your research and earn a doctorate in 1903.

64
00:05:53.583 --> 00:05:57.984
You were also awarded the Nobel Prize in Physics that year!

65
00:05:59.045 --> 00:06:02.426
I shared the prize with Henri Becquerel and my husband.

66
00:06:03.407 --> 00:06:08.729
Many people assumed that the two men had done most of the work, but my husband was sure

67
00:06:08.769 --> 00:06:10.169
to give me proper credit!

68
00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:16.472
Sadly, just three years later, Pierre died when he was struck by a horse-drawn wagon.

69
00:06:17.596 --> 00:06:21.099
I was devastated but had to carry on.

70
00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:25.783
We had two young daughters, and there was more work waiting in the lab.

71
00:06:26.684 --> 00:06:33.349
When the physics department at the Sorbonne offered me Pierre's job, I became the school's first female professor.

72
00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:36.571
You certainly did carry on, Dr.

73
00:06:36.632 --> 00:06:44.077
Curie! In 1911 you were awarded a second Nobel Prize, this time in chemistry.

74
00:06:45.058 --> 00:06:46.960
Then World War I broke out.

75
00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:50.041
That interrupted your research, right?

76
00:06:51.222 --> 00:06:56.225
By 1914 I had helped to establish the Radium Institute, a research center.

77
00:06:57.245 --> 00:07:00.987
But before I could begin working there, the war arrived.

78
00:07:02.048 --> 00:07:06.370
I soon found a new way to use my scientific background—and save lives.

79
00:07:07.431 --> 00:07:14.335
X-rays, which had been discovered in 1895, were being used to diagnose patients' injuries, but this equipment was

80
00:07:14.435 --> 00:07:16.216
only available at city hospitals.

81
00:07:17.388 --> 00:07:24.715
So I worked with the Red Cross to set up twenty cars with x-ray machines— "little Curies"—that could

82
00:07:24.735 --> 00:07:26.496
be driven right to the battlefield.

83
00:07:27.697 --> 00:07:31.681
I also set up two hundred field hospitals with radiography rooms.

84
00:07:32.801 --> 00:07:37.566
At first I didn't know how to use the x-ray technology, but I taught myself and trained other

85
00:07:37.626 --> 00:07:39.287
women to use the machines too.

86
00:07:40.348 --> 00:07:44.712
Thanks to you, over a million soldiers were x-rayed during the war.

87
00:07:45.696 --> 00:07:47.757
What did you do when the fighting was over?

88
00:07:48.857 --> 00:07:54.059
I went back to the Radium Institute, and later began touring the world and giving lectures to raise

89
00:07:54.119 --> 00:07:55.399
funds for the institute.

90
00:07:56.380 --> 00:08:00.981
But I was impatient with all the travel and longed to be back in my quiet lab.

91
00:08:01.962 --> 00:08:08.764
And by 1933 I'm afraid I often felt ill, probably due to years of working with radiation.

92
00:08:09.932 --> 00:08:12.813
We certainly don't want to wear you out here, Dr.

93
00:08:12.893 --> 00:08:15.534
Curie, so we'll let you return to history.

94
00:08:16.514 --> 00:08:19.735
Thank you for your contributions to science and medicine!

95
00:08:20.895 --> 00:08:22.536
I appreciate that, Faye.

96
00:08:22.956 --> 00:08:34.380
Good-bye. In 1934 Marie Curie's health was deteriorating, and she died that same year at the age of

97
00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:44.188
66. The cause of her death was long-term exposure to radioactive material, which likely came from her work

98
00:08:44.248 --> 00:08:46.709
with x-ray equipment during World War I.

99
00:08:46.729 --> 00:08:54.374
A pioneer in physics and chemistry, she paved the way for future female scientists.

100
00:08:55.454 --> 00:09:03.859
Her research also led to many technologies in energy, space, and medicine, especially the use of radiation to

101
00:09:03.979 --> 00:09:04.940
treat cancer.

102
00:09:06.148 --> 00:09:09.150
I hope you enjoyed my interview with Marie Curie.

103
00:09:10.131 --> 00:09:14.353
You've been watching Faye Moss on Superstars in History!
